+**The read law.** `get`/`get_tagged` is *a refcount-shared read, never a
+borrow*. The capability's app-compiled clone operation passes the cell's
+ownership unit of the stored `Box(a)` through its private typed share closure,
+which returns two units: `keep` is written back into the source cell and `out`
+is stored as the clone. A public get then consumes the clone. Both units are
+ordinarily the same box, retained once by compiled Roc, so a read allocates
+nothing. A shared box is not a borrow: each holder owns one unit of its count,
+and dropping either must not invalidate the other. A typed consumer that unboxes
+a shared box retains every nested refcounted field (`Str`, `List`, record, tag
+union, boxed closure) and releases only its own unit of the box, and any
+mutation sees those counts and copies. Isolation between holders therefore comes
+from the compiler's uniqueness checks rather than from distinct allocations. The
+host relies on this law but never enforces it by inspecting bytes; correctness
+is the capability's responsibility, expressed in typed Roc and lowered by the
+backend. This law depends on the compiler treating `Box.unbox` and box reuse as
+refcount-aware for a box with count above one, which is pinned by a test against
+each adopted compiler, not assumed.
+
+Because a box may have several owners, nothing may treat a cell's box as unique
+or its address as identity. Two cells holding one box are still two cells, each
+with its own handle, capability retain, and lifetime. Equality still calls
+`is_eq` on two aliases of one box; reflexivity is never assumed from the pointer.
+
+**Boundary discipline.** The host never walks a payload, never increments the
+refcount of a retained value's box or anything nested in it, and never
+identifies a value by pointer shape. Every ownership
 action — clone, compare, release — is a capability call. Typed aliasing
